# Target C — extreme-stress body-rigidity frontier

**LEVEL I STATUS: STILL OPEN.**

Full proof: [target_c_extreme_stress_body_rigidity_2026-08-31.md](../../reports/target_c_extreme_stress_body_rigidity_2026-08-31.md).

## C57 — every extreme null stress is a rigid body

Let `s=(alpha,beta)` be an extreme normalized KKT stress and let a fixed-diameter critical velocity `v` satisfy

`Omega_s V_x = Omega_s V_y = 0`.

Every positive support edge is strong, so its relative velocity is tangential:

`dv_e=kappa_e J r_e` on positive contacts,

`dv_h=omega_h J r_h` on positive diameter edges.

The vector kernel equation implies that

`(alpha_e kappa_e, beta_h omega_h)`

is another signed equilibrium stress on exactly the same support. Subtract the original stress times

`c=sum_h beta_h omega_h`.

The result is a signed equilibrium stress supported inside `supp(s)` whose diameter coefficients sum to zero. If it were nonzero, small positive and negative perturbations of `s` along this direction would remain normalized nonnegative KKT stresses, contradicting extremality.

Therefore every positive contact and positive diameter edge has angular rate `c`. The positive contact component is connected, so after subtracting `cJp` the velocity is one translation on the entire component. An extreme null stress is an infinitesimal rigid body.

## Null-face body cover

C54 gives an exposed KKT face `F_0` whose every stress annihilates the full infinitesimal-flex space `L` of the maximal strong framework.

Every extreme point of `F_0` is therefore a rigid body for every `v in L`.

Any two extreme positive contact components intersect in at least one source vertex. Otherwise their midpoint would be a normalized KKT stress with two nontrivial positive-contact components, contradicting support connectivity.

If two extreme bodies share two distinct source vertices, their rigid motions coincide and they can be merged.

Thus all remaining C-I flex is relative rotation of rigid null-face bodies at one-vertex intersections.

## Interaction with C56

Mix two null-face extreme stresses. Their midpoint stays in the null face.

If its two-dimensional transfer kernel has `min K_ef<0`, C56-G forces one common diameter angular rate on the entire midpoint support, so the two extreme bodies cannot rotate differently.

Therefore any pair of null-face extreme bodies that carries different angular rates has a C56-Z midpoint:

`K=B^{-1}`, `M=0`,

and the union positive diameter graph is a forest.

So surviving shear is now simultaneously:

- a one-vertex rigid-body hinge in the primal positive contact framework; and
- a global zero-transfer forest in the circular-planar response.

## Graph-cut bridge

Positive Kalmanson split weights of a connected circular-planar electrical network are exactly weights of actual displayed circular splits, computed from respecting spanning 2-groves. Hence C56-Z's one-edge/two-adjacent-edge split localization is a physical graph-cut restriction.

## Next exact target

Prove that a zero-transfer midpoint of two full-diameter extreme bodies meeting at one source vertex is either:

1. literally a one-sum of the two positive contact networks at that hinge vertex, in which case analyze a finite relative rotation; or
2. connected by another path, in which case derive a displayed split whose diameter-edge crossing set violates C56-Z locality.

Closing that body-hinge alternative closes Gate C-I through C7.
